  "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{\u2032}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\u2026,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$",
